🤖 An option to consider, not a blocker: key the hero layout by context length instead of using two free-standing constants.

As written, the 65K deploy sets `max_seq_len` in `HERO_MODEL` (`heuristic.py`) and must also flip `HERO_CONTEXT_AXIS_SIZE` and `HERO_BALANCE_ATTENTION_ROWS` in `hero_recipe.py`. If it forgets those two, every check passes and the 65K run trains at CP1. The PR's own motivation is that CP4 with row balancing is the better layout at 65K, so that fact can live next to the context length it applies to:

```python
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class HeroLayout:
    context_axis_size: int
    balance_attention_rows: bool

    @property
    def expert_axis_size(self) -> int:
        return hero_expert_axis_size(self.context_axis_size)


# The layout the hero trains with at each context length.
HERO_LAYOUTS = {
    4096: HeroLayout(context_axis_size=1, balance_attention_rows=False),
    16384: HeroLayout(context_axis_size=1, balance_attention_rows=False),
    65536: HeroLayout(context_axis_size=4, balance_attention_rows=True),
}


def hero_layout(seq_len: int) -> HeroLayout:
    if seq_len not in HERO_LAYOUTS:
        raise ValueError(f"no hero layout for seq_len={seq_len}; add one to HERO_LAYOUTS")
    return HERO_LAYOUTS[seq_len]
```

`build_ladder_run` would use `hero_layout(seq_len)` for d6144 and `HeroLayout(1, False)` for the narrow rungs. `hero_grug_trainer_config` and `hero_trainer_config` would take one `layout` instead of three loose kwargs.

| | Constants (this PR) | Layout table |
|---|---|---|
| 65K deploy edits | `max_seq_len`, plus 2 constants in `hero_recipe.py` | `max_seq_len` only |
| Deploy forgets CP | Trains silently at CP1 | Not possible |
| Diagnostic at `--seq-len 4096` after the deploy | Defaults to CP4 | Defaults to CP1, as the hero ran at 4K |
| Unplanned context (e.g. 262K) | Runs with whatever CP is checked in | Fails at launch until a row is added |
| Tests | Monkeypatch module constants | Read the table |

The cost is that the 65K row lands before the deploy instead of in it. The 16K config doesn't change, because its row is CP1.

@bethehand 
Thanks for the measurement. One correction for the comparison plan: my forecast B (1.935) is built only on the scaling-ladder rows, which were evaluated at 4K packing, so it is a 4K-built forecast like the Aug 19 one. Forecast A (1.979) is anchored on the hero's 16 evals at 16K. This means my registered H1 and H4, scored against the 16K final value, favour my forecasts by about 0.03 from the packing change alone. I will score H1 to H4 as registered and report packing-corrected versions next to them: 4K-built forecasts against Y + Δ, with Δ from a 4K eval of the final checkpoint if one is logged, otherwise your 0.0306 (0.0283 as a sensitivity check). [This is fixed now, before the outcome](https://github.com/mindscanlabs/NAI-track-record/blob/main/forecasts/marin-535b.md#notes-added-before-the-outcome-25-october-2026).

On the 262K phase: +1 for keeping all 16 Paloma subsets (GrugEvalConfig.max_seq_len = 65,536 per #9890, or masking zero-token tags in the top-level macro). A 14-subset final value would make every forecast in this thread incomparable. A single 4K eval of the final checkpoint would also let the 4K-built forecasts be checked directly.
